# Question 2: WSDL Structure for an Enterprise Web Service


# 2.2 Major Components of WSDL



Example:

```xml
<types>
    <xsd:schema
        xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">

        <xsd:element name="GetCustomerRequest">
            <xsd:complexType>
                <xsd:sequence>
                    <xsd:element name="customerId"
                                 type="xsd:int"/>
                </xsd:sequence>
            </xsd:complexType>
        </xsd:element>

    </xsd:schema>
</types>
```

---

## 2. Message

The `message` element defines the input and output messages exchanged by the service.

Example:

```xml
<message name="GetCustomerRequest">
    <part name="parameters"
          element="tns:GetCustomerRequest"/>
</message>

<message name="GetCustomerResponse">
    <part name="parameters"
          element="tns:GetCustomerResponse"/>
</message>
```

---

## 3. PortType

The `portType` defines the abstract interface of the service.

It specifies the operations supported by the service.

Example:

```xml
<portType name="CustomerPortType">

    <operation name="getCustomer">
        <input message="tns:GetCustomerRequest"/>
        <output message="tns:GetCustomerResponse"/>
    </operation>

    <operation name="updateCustomer">
        <input message="tns:UpdateCustomerRequest"/>
        <output message="tns:UpdateCustomerResponse"/>
    </operation>

</portType>
```

The `portType` can be compared to an interface in programming because it describes what the service can do without specifying how communication takes place.

---

## 2.4 Binding

The `binding` specifies how the abstract operations are implemented over a communication protocol.

For example, the service can use SOAP over HTTP.

```xml
<binding name="CustomerSoapBinding"
         type="tns:CustomerPortType">

    <soap:binding
        style="document"
        transport="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/http"/>

</binding>
```

The binding therefore connects the abstract interface with a concrete communication protocol.

---

## 2.5 Service

The `service` element specifies the actual endpoint where the web service can be accessed.

```xml
<service name="CustomerService">

    <port name="CustomerPort"
          binding="tns:CustomerSoapBinding">

        <soap:address
            location="https://example.com/services/customer"/>

    </port>

</service>
```

The endpoint tells the client where to send SOAP messages.

---

# 2.6 Complete Simplified WSDL Structure

The following represents the overall structure:

```xml
<definitions
    name="CustomerService"
    targetNamespace="http://example.com/customer"
    xmlns:tns="http://example.com/customer"
    xmlns:soap="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/wsdl/soap/"
    xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">

    <types>

        <xsd:schema
            targetNamespace="http://example.com/customer">

            <xsd:element name="GetCustomerRequest">
                <xsd:complexType>
                    <xsd:sequence>
                        <xsd:element name="customerId"
                                     type="xsd:int"/>
                    </xsd:sequence>
                </xsd:complexType>
            </xsd:element>

        </xsd:schema>

    </types>

    <message name="GetCustomerRequest">
        <part name="parameters"
              element="tns:GetCustomerRequest"/>
    </message>

    <message name="GetCustomerResponse">
        <part name="parameters"
              element="tns:GetCustomerResponse"/>
    </message>

    <portType name="CustomerPortType">

        <operation name="getCustomer">
            <input message="tns:GetCustomerRequest"/>
            <output message="tns:GetCustomerResponse"/>
        </operation>

    </portType>

    <binding name="CustomerSoapBinding"
             type="tns:CustomerPortType">

        <soap:binding
            style="document"
            transport="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/http"/>

    </binding>

    <service name="CustomerService">

        <port name="CustomerPort"
              binding="tns:CustomerSoapBinding">

            <soap:address
                location="https://example.com/customer"/>

        </port>

    </service>

</definitions>
```

---

# 2.7 WSDL Component Relationship

The components can be understood as follows:

```text
              WSDL
               |
       +-------+-------+
       |               |
     Types           Messages
       |               |
       +-------+-------+
               |
            PortType
               |
            Binding
               |
             Service
               |
           Endpoint URL
```

The flow is:

**Types → Messages → PortType → Binding → Service → Endpoint**

---

# 2.8 Importance of WSDL in Service Integration

WSDL is important because it:

1. Provides a standardized service description.
2. Allows clients to discover available operations.
3. Defines input and output data structures.
4. Specifies communication protocols.
5. Specifies the service endpoint.
6. Supports interoperability between different platforms.
7. Enables automated client generation.
8. Reduces integration errors.
9. Separates service interface from implementation.
10. Makes enterprise systems easier to integrate.

For example, a Java client, .NET client, or another programming language can consume the same WSDL and communicate with the service.

Thus, WSDL acts as a **contract between the service provider and service consumer**.

